nt for the naga pro  is of course the three panels that it  comes with so you can choose between six  buttons  two buttons or of course 12 buttons  these will attach to the main body of  the mouse with a nice satisfying  click and their whole purpose is that  they're designed around different genres  of games  with a two button option for first  person shooters a 12 button option  for things like mobas mmos and a six  button option  based around the battle royale genre i  will say i did wonder how much use  i'd get out of these i'd come from a  mouse where i'd only had  two buttons and i did immediately find  it quite tricky  to find 12 small buttons with my fat  thumb  but as i started to experiment i found  more and more  use and more and more utility out of  these panels  going from using my number pad to six  buttons on the side of my mouse  was a little bit of a learning curve but  once i was there i did honestly find it  really intuitive quite easy to use and  made me want to  experiment and kind of push and see what  else i could do with them  so in escape from tarkov i was able to  use my med kits that little bit quicker  in dota 2 i was much more confident in  using my active items  and i thought um i can use these buttons  to get to the build mode  in fortnite a bit quicker and maybe i'll  be able to compete with all the  sweaty school children that dominate  that game  didn't work but i must say it did all  give me confidence to start seeing how  far i could push the extra utility of  these buttons  so i would assign them hotkeys in things  like adobe premiere  and it would improve my workflow  somewhat  but it also did a strange mental thing  where i would go from  the two button panel and that was like  gaming mode  to the six button panel it was like work  mode  so i was that was how i differentiated  my my work time  and my game time with mouse buttons  so what i suppose i'm trying to say is  that they really are as useful  as you make them out to be 