p i had it in  whether i was gaming no matter how much  time i gave it  i really couldn't stop noticing the  weight of the junk  in this thing's trunk to its credit  though it still has really great build  quality  it's sturdy there's no flex in the  plastic but you are getting  normal mechanical switches with a normal  clicky button  but again that's one of the reasons why  the price point for the rival 3  is so much lower but there are people  out there that prefer that tactile  mechanical feel  and don't care what discord server they  disrupt with it  there is more to be said on the subject  of connecting these things to your  computer though  with both of them being able to use 2.4  gigahertz wireless with the included  dongle  and razer utilizing their hyper speed  tech but when it comes to bluetooth  the rival 3 does take a hit  the performance hit the rival 3 takes on  bluetooth mode is severe enough  that i would say it's almost a different  mouse i can't recommend using it  on bluetooth for long periods of gaming  when you're in bluetooth mode you're  forced to their default bluetooth  settings  it takes that 1000hz polling rate and  forces you down  to 125 and in bluetooth mode you also  miss out  on any acceleration or angle snapping  features that you may have enabled it  kind of neuters the gaming out of this  gaming mouse  now using these things on bluetooth is  going to prolong the battery life  or the time between having to swap out  those double a's  but in my opinion you've got a lot of  power in both of these  free up a usb slot put the dongle in and  get what you're paying for  they do have some battery saving options  to make those charges  go a little longer the idle time can be  turned up and down so that if you leave  your  mouse to one side for a little while  it'll turn itself off and the rival 3  when you're using it and moving around  will disable the rgb  because your hands on it the razer has  quite  a lot of rgb but it will remain  illuminated as you're using the mouse  but steelseries said what do we need to  illuminate your middle finger for dave  which turn off  save those double a's now of course to  play with all those rgb  and power saving settings you're going  to have to use the software that comes  with the mouse  because it's current year and that's how  mice work now i will say though that  razer synapse is much more feature-rich  it has more utility when it comes to  remapping all the buttons monitoring  battery  changing your lights and it was all  extremely easy and i was extremely  impressed with the amount of options  that i had when it came to the  customization utility  basically allowing me to set up any  macro or shortcut that i wanted  off the back of the 19 plus buttons on  the naga pro  the steel series engine is still a great  utility and does allow for some  flexibility when it comes to changing  your settings for power management its  sensitivities and  using in the built-in acceleration angle  snapping features but there is  definitely less utility when it comes to  macros and remapping  but honestly still more than enough  there for the majority of gamers  but when it comes to which one i think  is most worth it  it's honestly closer than i thought it  would be  in the case of the naga pro it feels  like a specialized  tool for whatever game i'm playing you  have to put a little work  to get it there but once you get the  buttons to what you want them to be  you get comfortable with the side panel  it really does  feel like a mouse that was built for a  specific genre but you have the option  to change genre it's like having three  mice almost  whereas in the case of the rival 3 with  no offense meant  it's just a mouse a really really good  mouse a great mouse  but in comparison it is just a mouse  but does all of that warrant the massive  price difference between these two  honestly great pieces of kit well  in the case of the naga pro again going  back to the side panel issue  i'm only ever going to use two of them  so it is a source of frustration knowing  that there is a piece  of plastic and circuitry that's worth  good money  sitting in my drawer but with all that  being said it's so  comfortable it performs so well if i was  being honest with myself it probably is  my favorite  mouse of all time but that high price  point  really is a sticking issue when it comes  to the rival 3 though you really  are getting quite a lot for your money  sure you're going to have to swap the  batteries out  it might not be as feature rich as the  naga pro but  for an entry-level wireless mouse or  something for the mid-range gamer  or someone who just wants to try the  wireless life on a bit more of a budget  i think this one is most worth it if  you're someone that wants those high-end  peripherals has the money to spare  if you're playing lots and lots of games  across genres or just wants the best  gaming mouse money can buy  maybe you'll go for the naga pro because  i do think  this is the best gaming mouse on the  market right now  but i think the rival 3 is more worth it  if either sound like that'd be up your  street then you can use the affiliate  link in the description below to get one  for yourself  if you've used either of these in the  past let me know your thoughts in the  comments section down below  and please tell us what things we should  compare next keyboards  monitors headsets the world's our oyster  so be sure to subscribe so you don't  miss out on any of those like the video  let us know if you've enjoyed and we'll  see you in the next one  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

